Hi Liz,Thank you.I have also tried to stratify on two variables, to created 21 stratifications, and then do matching within each stratification with only two variables as covariates, but the memory problem still exists, both on my computer and other computer. I don't think it's meaningful to split the dataset into more stratifications. Problem still exists because the sample sizes of several sub datasets are still large ( can be 10,000+). The program runs well with smaller datasets.  Hi Gary,Thank you for your advice, I will look at the CEM package.  Hi Kosuke,Thank you. I tried that, but it couldn't help. Memory problem is very common in R when analyzing large datasets, even on computer with lots of RAM. I also faced this problem when doing multiple imputations on this dataset. I know there are some packages in R allowing users to manage and analyze datasets larger than available RAM and share them across nodes of a cluster. But I didn't find one which can be applied in Matc
 hIt. Anyone who knows what package can I use to deal with this problem?All your help will be greatly appreciated. Best,XinFrom: kimai at Princeton.EDU
